Concrete’s carbonation involves the ingress of gaseous (acidic) CO 2  into the pore structure, wherein it dissolves into the highly alkaline pore solution (pH > 12.5)—steadily reducing the solution pH as carbonation progresses. Within the pore solution, dissolved CO 2  species (CO 3 2−  anions at prevailing pH) react with aqueous Ca 2+  cations to form CaCO 3 . This process is often accompanied by the release of silicate species from the decalcification of C–S–H, resulting in the precipitation of amorphous silica phases 27 , 28 , 53 , 110 ,  111 ,  112 ,  113 ,  114 ,  115 ,  116 ,  117 ,  118 – 119 . The calcium ions in the pore solution originate from dissolution of the clinker phases—such as alite, belite, aluminate, ferrite, and gypsum—and remain in sufficiently high concentrations so as to sustain supersaturation with respect to the hydration products like portlandite and calcium silicate hydrate 27 , 110 . As calcium cations are consumed via carbonation, key hydration products—most notably portlandite and C-S-H—are progressively depleted or “decalcified.” This decalcification process enables carbonation to proceed, provided that a continuous supply of both CO₂ and accessible calcium-bearing phases are available 27 , 28 , 110 ,  111 ,  112 ,  113 ,  114 ,  115 ,  116 ,  117 ,  118 – 119 . The prominent carbonation reactions are shown in Eqs.  2 and  3 , which, together, result in the uptake of 0.2–0.6 g CO2 /g solid  (g CO2 /g s ) 114 ,  115 ,  116 ,  117 ,  118 – 119 . 2 x C a O . y S i O 2 . n H 2 O + x C O 2 → x C a C O 3 + y S i O 2 + n H 2 O 0.23 − 0.39 g CO 2 / g s \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
